seg-e666f1eb7a59 = { "1" } of { "6" } bars passes. The cause is geometry. No source publishes the constellation's orbit elements. On the ILLUSTRATIVE in-tree elements, a user and the station share four satellites above { "5" } deg in only { "8" } % ({ "300" } km) and { "6" } % ({ "600" } km) of epochs, at near-singular geometry: the uncorrected p95 is { "3.8" } km, where the sources quote < { "10" } m. The sources' own simulations used a filter, a height constraint and three satellites in common view, which this snapshot model does not have. The run therefore does not reproduce the claims on those elements, and it is not evidence that the claims are wrong. The K6 pass is a ratio of two geometry-dominated figures and says nothing about decorrelation. A published set of the constellation's elements would be scored under the same bars as a second, dated run.

